@tests

// What the table refuses, asserted in the program rather than beside it.
//
// The run in `main.sysl` can only assert what the table *permits*: a violated `require` traps and
// takes the process with it, so a check that broke one would end the run instead of reporting a
// failure. `@test(should_trap)` is the form that can — each runs in a process of its own and passes
// by **not** coming back.
//
// **The refusal is written next to the call that is not refused.** A `should_trap` test passes for
// any failure at all, its own setup included, so alone it cannot tell "the contract fired" from
// "nothing worked". One row up to the column count and one row past it, and the difference between
// them is the contract.

@test(should_trap)
a_row_with_more_cells_than_columns_is_refused() =
    var t = table([Left, Right])

    t.add(["a", 1, "one too many"])

@test(should_trap)
and_so_is_one_with_fewer() =
    var t = table([Left, Right])

    t.add(["a"])

// The call the two above are measured against: the same table, a row of exactly two, which goes in.
@test
a_row_of_the_right_width_is_taken() =
    var t = table([Left, Right])

    t.add(["a", 1])

    assert_eq(t.rows(), 1usize, "the row went in")

// A table of one column is the degenerate case and is still a table — worth pinning because the
// stride walk in `width` reads `ncols` as its step, and a step of one is where an off-by-one in it
// would not show.
@test
one_column_is_still_a_table() =
    var t = table([Left])

    t.add(["only"])
    t.add(["a"])

    assert_eq(t.width(0usize), 4usize, "the header is the widest cell")
    assert_eq(t.rows(), 2usize, "both rows are there")

// And a column whose header is the widest cell, which is the case a table that measured only the
// data would get wrong.
@test
the_header_is_measured_with_the_data() =
    var t = table([Left])

    t.add(["a rather long header"])
    t.add(["x"])

    assert_eq(t.width(0usize), 20usize, "the header sets the width")
